#' Trust and stress in three countries (simulated)
#'
#' Simulated survey data used in the examples and tests. Two correlated
#' factors, trust and stress, are each measured by four 5-point Likert items
#' in three countries. Loadings are equal in all countries, but item `stress3`
#' has a shifted intercept in Brazil, so loading invariance holds and
#' intercept invariance does not. Twelve values per item are missing at random.
#' The data are generated by `tools/make_examples.R`.
#'
#' @format A data frame with 1200 rows and 9 variables:
#' \describe{
#'   \item{country}{Factor: Chile (n = 420), Brazil (n = 380), Spain (n = 400).}
#'   \item{trust1, trust2, trust3, trust4}{Ordered factors with levels 1 to 5.}
#'   \item{stress1, stress2, stress3, stress4}{Ordered factors with levels 1 to 5.}
#' }
"wizdata"

#' A laboratory of instruments, good and bad (simulated)
#'
#' One simulated data set with several short instruments that differ in item
#' type (ordinal, numeric, binary) and in quality, so that every analysis and
#' figure of psyWiz has something to show. What each block was built to show,
#' and what to try on it, is described in `docs/DATA.md` in the package
#' repository. The data are generated by `tools/make_examples.R`.
#'
#' @format A data frame with 1500 rows and 49 variables:
#' \describe{
#'   \item{country}{Factor: North, Centre, South (500 each).}
#'   \item{calm1 ... calm5}{Ordered, 1 to 5. A clean one-factor scale.}
#'   \item{focus1 ... focus6}{Ordered, 1 to 5. Correlates .5 with Calm.
#'     `focus1` also loads on Calm, `focus2` is easier to endorse in the
#'     South, `focus4` barely measures anything, `focus5` and `focus6` share
#'     wording (a residual correlation).}
#'   \item{speed1 ... speed4}{Numeric. Tau-equivalent: equal loadings,
#'     unequal error variances.}
#'   \item{power1 ... power4}{Numeric. Very unequal loadings and variances.}
#'   \item{body1 ... body4, mind1 ... mind4, social1 ... social4}{Ordered, 1
#'     to 5. Wellbeing: a general factor plus three specific factors.}
#'   \item{know1 ... know6}{0/1. A near-perfect Guttman scale, from the
#'     easiest to the hardest item.}
#'   \item{habit1 ... habit5}{0/1. Not a scale: weak items, one unrelated,
#'     one reversed.}
#'   \item{debt_family, debt_friends, debt_bank, debt_card, debt_lender,
#'     debt_mortgage}{0/1. Sources of debt forming a partial order: family
#'     and friends on the same rung, bank debt builds on them and card debt
#'     on bank debt, a moneylender builds on informal debt but not on bank
#'     debt, and a mortgage is unrelated to all of it.}
#' }
"wizlab"
